# CodeChecker skip list for kernel (device) code analysis.
# https://codechecker.readthedocs.io/en/latest/analyzer/user_guide/#skip
#
# Used with --drop-reports-from-skipped-files, so these paths are excluded from
# BOTH analysis and reporting. Unlike clang-tidy's HeaderFilterRegex this also
# suppresses clang-diagnostic-* findings, which is the point: the SFPI headers
# emit parse noise we cannot act on here.
#
# Exclusion-only, same shape as tt-umd's .codechecker.skiplist. Note that an
# allow-list keyed on kernels/ would analyze nothing at all: the translation
# units are the firmware wrappers
# (tt_metal/hw/firmware/src/.../{trisck,brisck,ncrisck,idle_erisck}.cc) with the
# kernel #included into them, so no TU path contains kernels/. Anything not
# listed here is analyzed, which fails open for in-repo code as new device
# directories appear.

# Upstream toolchain: pinned SFPI release and the host libc/libstdc++ used for
# the analysis parse. Not fixable in this repo.
-/opt/tenstorrent/sfpi/*
-/usr/*

# The machine-generated JIT glue in the kernel cache (chlkc_*.cpp prologs, the
# generated kernel_main() shim, chlkc_descriptors.h format tables) is analyzed
# deliberately: a generator that emits bad code is still a bug worth filing.
# Two consequences to expect in the report. Cache paths embed the kernel name
# and two content hashes, so one generator defect appears once per kernel
# variant rather than collapsing under deduplication; and the cache lives on the
# test runner while only plists are uploaded, so these findings have no
# browsable source in the rendered report. Both are accepted for now — see
# tech_reports/code-indexing/kernel-clang-tidy.md.
